<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tomara dynasty of Delhi</h1><p class="st-meta">736 CE – 1152 CE (approx.) · capital Dhillika</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Rajput dynasty traditionally credited with founding Delhi as Dhillika and building its first citadel, Lal Kot.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bardic tradition says Anangapala I founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Dhillika</a>, the first city of Delhi, in 736. Historically, the Tomaras appear in the 9th and 10th centuries as chiefs in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a> region, probably feudatories of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara</a>. Their rule at Delhi is attested mainly by later inscriptions and literary sources.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anangapala-ii">Anangapala II</a> is credited with building the fortified citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lal-kot">Lal Kot</a> in the 11th century, the oldest identifiable city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and an inscription on the Iron Pillar names him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surajkund">Surajkund</a>, a stepped reservoir near Delhi, is traditionally attributed to a Tomara king. They ruled a modest kingdom and were repeatedly attacked by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavids</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the mid-12th century the Tomaras were defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vigraharaja-iv">Vigraharaja IV</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chahamanas-of-shakambhari">Chahamanas of Shakambhari</a>, who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>; Tomara chiefs continued there as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chahamana</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> until the Ghurid conquest of 1192.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tomara-dynasty-of-delhi.jpg" alt="Tomara dynasty of Delhi" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Roboture · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Outer_Wall_of_Lal_kot_and_Rai_Pithora.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>736 CE – 1152 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dhillika</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Anangapala II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit, Apabhramsha</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tomaras of Delhi, Tomaras of Dhillika</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=944&amp;focus=tomara-dynasty-of-delhi" aria-label="Tomara dynasty of Delhi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6756 1340 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tomara dynasty of Delhi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7103 1442h0M6969 1462h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7076 1596h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7076 1596h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day India. <a href="/atlas?year=944&amp;focus=tomara-dynasty-of-delhi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

